## What This Rule Does

`flutter_bloc` is the state-management approach for this app. This rule keeps
state management consistent: the right unit of state, sealed event and state
hierarchies, blocs scoped correctly, and `setState` reserved for what it's
actually for.

> **Divergence from the upstream platform.** `flutter-platform` ships this
> rule in a Riverpod form. This app deliberately uses BLoC, so this rule,
> `04-flutter-test-guard.mdc`, and the `flutter-bloc-gen` / `flutter-screen-gen`
> / `flutter-test-gen` skills were rewritten. Do not "restore" Riverpod
> guidance from the upstream copy — see `memory-bank/architecture.md`.

## Bloc, Not Cubit

This codebase uses **event-driven `Bloc` everywhere**, not `Cubit`. Every
state transition is triggered by an event, so the sequence of user intent is
traceable and replayable in tests and in `onTransition` logging.

```dart
// ❌ Cubit — no event record of why state changed
class MenuCubit extends Cubit<MenuState> {
  Future<void> load() async { ... }
}

// ✓ Bloc — the intent is a first-class, testable value
class MenuBloc extends Bloc<MenuEvent, MenuState> {
  MenuBloc({required MenuRepository repository})
      : _repository = repository,
        super(const MenuInitial()) {
    on<MenuRequested>(_onRequested);
    on<MenuCategorySelected>(_onCategorySelected);
  }

  final MenuRepository _repository;
  ...
}
```

Do not introduce a `Cubit` alongside these blocs — a single pattern is the
point. If a feature's state genuinely has no meaningful events, it probably
isn't bloc-worthy state at all (see `setState` Boundaries below).

## Sealed Events, Sealed State Variants

Events and states are both **sealed class hierarchies** extending
`Equatable`. Sealed types give exhaustive `switch` checking in the UI; the
`Equatable` base makes `bloc_test`'s `expect: [...]` comparisons work by
value.

```dart
sealed class MenuEvent extends Equatable {
  const MenuEvent();
  @override
  List<Object?> get props => const [];
}

final class MenuRequested extends MenuEvent {
  const MenuRequested();
}

final class MenuCategorySelected extends MenuEvent {
  const MenuCategorySelected(this.categoryId);
  final String categoryId;

  @override
  List<Object?> get props => [categoryId];
}
```

```dart
sealed class MenuState extends Equatable {
  const MenuState();
  @override
  List<Object?> get props => const [];
}

final class MenuInitial extends MenuState {
  const MenuInitial();
}

final class MenuLoadInProgress extends MenuState {
  const MenuLoadInProgress();
}

final class MenuLoadSuccess extends MenuState {
  const MenuLoadSuccess({required this.drinks, this.selectedCategoryId});
  final List<Drink> drinks;
  final String? selectedCategoryId;

  @override
  List<Object?> get props => [drinks, selectedCategoryId];
}

final class MenuLoadFailure extends MenuState {
  const MenuLoadFailure(this.message);
  final String message;

  @override
  List<Object?> get props => [message];
}
```

Rules for these hierarchies:

- Every event and state class is `final` (or `sealed` if further extended)
  and immutable — `const` constructors wherever the fields allow.
- **Every state variant must be handled in the UI.** A `switch` over a sealed
  state with no `default` gives a compile error when a variant is added; keep
  it that way rather than adding a catch-all `default` branch.
- A failure state carries a **display-ready message**, not a raw exception.
  Mapping `AppException` → message happens in the bloc, so the widget never
  formats an error.
- Never expose a mutable collection in a state without `List.unmodifiable`
  or treating it as read-only; `Equatable`'s `props` compares by value, and a
  mutated list silently breaks change detection.

## Emit Discipline

- Emit a new state object; never mutate the current one. `MenuLoadSuccess`
  with a changed field is a new instance (`copyWith` on the entity, new state).
- `emit` is only valid inside an `on<Event>` handler. Do not capture `emit`
  and call it later from a callback or a `Future.then` — use `await` inside
  the handler, or `emit.forEach`/`emit.onEach` for a stream source.
- Never call `emit` after the bloc is closed. If a handler awaits something
  long-running, guard with `if (isClosed) return;` before emitting.
- Wrap every repository call in `try`/`catch` on the app's typed
  `AppException` and emit an explicit failure state — an uncaught throw in a
  handler surfaces as an unhandled `BlocUnhandledErrorException`, not as UI.

```dart
Future<void> _onRequested(MenuRequested event, Emitter<MenuState> emit) async {
  emit(const MenuLoadInProgress());
  try {
    final drinks = await _repository.fetchMenu();
    if (isClosed) return;
    emit(MenuLoadSuccess(drinks: drinks));
  } on AppException catch (e) {
    emit(MenuLoadFailure(e.message));
  }
}
```

- Use `transformer: droppable()`/`restartable()` from `bloc_concurrency` only
  if that package is added deliberately; without it, handlers for the same
  event type run concurrently. For a refresh-style event, guard re-entry with
  a state check instead of assuming serial execution.

## BlocProvider Scoping

- A bloc used by exactly one feature is provided at **that feature's route**
  (`BlocProvider(create: ...)` in the route's `builder`), not app-globally —
  this is what makes its state reset when the user leaves the flow.
- A bloc genuinely shared app-wide (`AuthBloc`) is provided once above
  `MaterialApp.router` in `MultiBlocProvider`. Most blocs are not app-wide;
  confirm against `memory-bank/architecture.md` before adding one there.
- Repositories are injected via `RepositoryProvider` at the app root and read
  with `context.read<XRepository>()` in a route's `create:` callback. A bloc
  takes its repository as a **constructor dependency on the abstract
  interface** — never constructs `FakeMenuRepository()` itself, which is what
  keeps it testable.
- Avoid module-level mutable singletons and global bloc instances. If you're
  tempted to reach for a global, a `RepositoryProvider`/`BlocProvider` covers
  it.
- `BlocProvider.value` is for re-exposing an **existing** bloc to a new
  subtree (e.g. a pushed modal). It does not own the bloc and will not close
  it — never use it to "share" a bloc you actually created elsewhere for
  convenience.

## Reading State

- `BlocBuilder`/`context.watch<XBloc>()` inside `build()` for reactive reads.
- `context.read<XBloc>()` inside event handlers (`onPressed`, `onSubmitted`)
  to dispatch. Calling `watch` in a callback rebuilds nothing and is a sign
  the wrong method was used; calling `read` in `build()` freezes the UI at
  the first state.
- `BlocSelector` or `buildWhen` when a widget only cares about one field of a
  large state — a whole-screen `BlocBuilder` that rebuilds on every unrelated
  field change is the most common avoidable rebuild in a bloc codebase.
- **`BlocListener` for side effects**, never `BlocBuilder`. Navigation,
  snackbars, and dialogs go in `listener:`; `builder:` must be a pure
  function of state. Navigating from inside `builder:` fires on every rebuild.
- `BlocConsumer` when a widget genuinely needs both, rather than nesting a
  listener inside a builder.

## `setState` Boundaries

`setState` is acceptable only for state that is:

- Local to a single widget (never read by a sibling or ancestor)
- Ephemeral (does not need to survive the widget being rebuilt from a bloc
  state change, does not need to be tested independently of the widget)

Fine: a `TextField`'s obscure-password toggle, an `ExpansionTile`'s
open/closed flag, a local `AnimationController` listener triggering a repaint.

Not fine: form validation state, anything persisted, list data from a
repository, anything another widget reacts to — these go through a bloc.

## Relationship to Other Rules

`01-flutter-architecture-guard.mdc` governs *where* blocs live and forbids
business rules inside them (pure rules belong in `domain/usecases/`); this
rule governs *which* bloc shape to use and *how* its state is emitted and
read. `04-flutter-test-guard.mdc` covers how these blocs are tested.
